To investigate whether the enzymes infiltrate into the asprepared protein trap, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption experiment was first conducted. As shown in Fig. S14 in the ESM, upon immobilization GOx, the surface area and pore volume of CoBDC-Fc are dramatically reduced. However, the variation of the surface area is higher than that of pore volume for the CoBDC system. This phenomenon suggests that the GOx with a large size \((6.0\;\mathrm{nm}\) \(\times\,5.2\,\mathrm{nm}\times7.7\,\mathrm{nm})\) may infiltrate into mesoporous \((>5\;\mathrm{nm})\) and adsorb on the surface in CoBDC-Fc simultaneously. Nevertheless, the pore of CoBDC is not big enough to encapsulate GOx, leading
to that the GOx prefers to adsorb on the surface of CoBDC. Then, the recycling experiments of MOF/enzymes show that the leaching amounts of enzymes in CoBDC-Fc/enzymes are lower than that of CoBDC/enzymes when the size of enzymes (e.g., HRP and GOx) is smaller than the pore of CoBDC-Fc but larger than the pore of CoBDC (Fig. S15 in the ESM). According to the previously reported works [24, 39], it is believed that small-size enzymes (Cyt-C, HRP, and GOx) prefer to infiltrate into the defect mesoporous in CoBDC-Fc, which can provide more intermolecular forces to stabilize enzymes (Fig. S16(a) in the ESM). Besides, the surface absorption of enzymes is inevitable. As for CoBDC, enzymes tend to absorb on its surface because the CoBDC lacks defective structures. However, when the pore size is smaller than that of the size of enzymes (e.g., CAT), the enzyme prefers to adsorb on the surface of CoBDC-Fc, and the advantages of protein trap are covered (Fig. S16(b) in the ESM).
## 3.3 Evaluation of properties of enzymes
To study the effect of MOFs on the performance of enzymes, the bioactivity of enzymes was evaluated systematically before and after immobilization. As shown in Figs. 3(a)–3(c), the catalytic reaction rate and efficiency of CoBDC-Fc/enzymes are superior to those of equivalent free enzymes, while the catalytic activity of CoBDC/enzymes bears a comparison with the free enzymes. On the other hand, both MOF/CAT composites present higher bioactivity than free CAT (Fig. 3(d)). Based on these, it is believed
that the 2D CoBDC with a thin size can circumvent the influence of mass transfer resistance of three-dimensional (3D) structure MOFs [40], thereby maintaining the catalytic efficiency of enzymes. To uncover the reason for the enhanced catalytic activity, the enzyme-like activity of MOFs was evaluated, which are expected to act as enzyme mimics (named nanozymes) due to the rich metal sites. Interestingly, the CoBDC-Fc expresses POD-like and CAT-like activities (Fig. S17 in the ESM), while the CoBDC only presents CAT-mimicking catalytic property. Through a comprehensive comparison of the activity of CoBDC-Fc, free enzymes, and CoBDC-Fc/enzyme composites, it can be concluded that the enhanced activity of MOF/enzyme (Cyt-C, HRP, and CAT) composites can be attributed to the cocatalyst effect of MOFs, providing the additional enzyme-like activity of MOFs catalytic performance (Fig. 3(e) and Fig. S18 in the ESM).
The reason for the enhanced activity of CoBDC-Fc/GOx is still unclear. Given that the microenvironment of MOFs is one of the key factors affecting the intrinsic activity of enzymes, the secondary structure of enzymes was studied via the FT-IR
the microenvironment adjacent to GOx, which induces the exposure or reversal of amino acid groups buried in the hydrophobic region of GOx [43,44]. Besides, the reduced and redshift fluorescence signals after treatment with Fc or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) further confirm the above results (Fig. S21(b) in the ESM). In GOx, flavin-adenine-dinitricotide (FAD) acts as the active center located at the deep and narrow channel in the catalytic pocket and is covered by a series of amino acid residue lids [45]. Therefore, it is believed that the increment in amino acid exposure may promote the transformation into open-lid conformation, improving the affinity between substrates and FAD. To confirm this assumption, the steady-state kinetic experiments of MOF/GOx and GOx were further performed. As shown in Fig. S22 and Table S5 in the ESM, the maximum reaction rate \(\left(V_{\mathrm{max}}\right)\) and Michaelis constant \(\left(K_{\mathrm{m}}\right)\) of CoBDC/GOx almost remain unchanged in comparison with the free GOx, indicating that CoBDC has no effect on the catalytic kinetics of GOx. Notably, compared with free GOx, the enhanced \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) value and the significantly decreased \(K_{\mathrm{m}}\) value suggest that CoBDC-Fc/GOx possesses superior reaction rates and affinity toward glucose (Fig. 4(b)). Integrating the above-mentioned results, we summed that the existence of Fc is beneficial for the communication between substrates and active sites, enhancing the catalytic efficiency of enzymes. Furthermore, the relative contents of secondary structures in GOx were calculated to obtain detailed information on conformation change. As listed in Table S6 in the ESM, the \(\alpha\)-helix content decreases, and the \(\beta\)-sheet and anti-parallel \(\beta\)-sheet contents increase after immobilization within CoBDC-Fc. Given the boosted activity, it is believed that these changes may contribute to optimizing the conformation of enzymes for substrate catalysis. Apart from the allosteric effect, Fc with redox-active property has been widely applied to serve as an electronic mediator to transfer reducing or oxidizing intermediates and improve the electron transfer of GOx [46, 47], thus enhancing the catalytic efficiency of the composite system. As illustrated in Fig. S23 in the ESM, Fc remains the function of electronic mediate after being introduced into CoBDC. Therefore, it is believed that the spatially proximal Fc may act as a cofactor unit to
synergetically promote the catalytic activity of immobilized GOx (Fqs. S(1)–S(3) in the ESM).
As a protein trap, protecting the enzyme from the external stimulus is a critical performance. Here, the stability of enzyme in extreme environments was evaluated. As shown in Fig. 4(c) and Fig. S24 in the ESM, the obtained MOF/GOx still possesses bioactivity when exposed to denaturing conditions (such as high temperature, strong acid, alkaline, and organic solvent). In contrast, the activity of free GOx dramatically diminished under the above harsh conditions. Importantly, the tolerability of CoBDC-Fc/GOx is superior to CoBDC/GOx due to the protein trap with stronger interaction forces confining GOx to maintain the conformation against the external stimulus [24]. Furthermore, the recyclability of GOx in different systems was investigated (Fig. 4(d)). The catalytic activity of CoBDC-Fc/GOx retained \(89\%\) after four cycles, while the CoBDC/GOx system possesses a \(24\%\) reduction in enzymatic activity. Taken together, the Fc-engineered protein trap can not only construct appropriated anchor sites to increase the enzyme immobilization efficiency but also endow the protein trap with additional functions, such as the modulation of enzyme conformation and the introduction of cofactor and cocatalysts, boosting the catalytic efficiency of immobilized systems (Fig. 4(e)). As shown in the radar map, the largest area of CoBDC-Fc reflects the superiority of the protein trap strategy for enzyme immobilization in the aspects of loading amounts, bioactivity, recyclability, tolerance, and stability (Fig. 4(f)).
