Prostate cancer is a major cancerous disease in men. Rapidly and sensitive monitoring of the disease is great of important [51]. Therefore, by virtue of the protein trap structure, the as-prepared CoBDC-Fc/GOx composites were further applied to immobilize the antibody of PSA for the construction of the ELISA platform (Fig. 5(a)). With the increase of PSA concentration, the absorbance at \(652\ \mathrm{nm}\) gradually increases and shows a good positive linear relationship in the range of \(5{-}800\ \mathrm{pg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (Fig. 5(c) and Fig. S33 in the ESM). And the limit of detection is calculated to be \(0.52\ \mathrm{pg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\). Furthermore, the proposed ELISA presents superior sensitivity in comparison with other previous methods (Table S11 in the ESM). The interference experiment verifies the
good selectivity of the CoBDC-Fc/GOx-based biosensor (Fig. 5(d)). Furthermore, the PSA concentrations of practical serum samples (two healthy persons and two patients) were monitored. As summarized in Table S12 in the ESM, the results are in good agreement with the test result of the hospital. These findings suggested that our proposed biosensor is expected to sensitively and accurately monitor the concentration of PSA in practice.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, a missing-linker strategy was used to simultaneously introduce abundant defects and redox-active Fc sites in CoBDC. Taking advantage of the augmented pore sizes, CoBDC-Fc shows a good ability to anchor enzymes, and the concept of protein trap is firstly proposed for enzyme immobilization. Importantly, the Fc-functionalized protein trap with enzyme-like activities enables synergistic improvement of the biocatalytic efficiency. Moreover, the hydrophobicity Fc can change the conformation of enzymes and serve as a cofactor to facilitate electron transfer, boosting the catalytic activity of CoBDC-Fc/GOx composites. Protected by the exoskeleton of MOFs, the immobilized enzymes possess excellent stability in inhospitable conditions and remarkable recyclability. Furthermore, benefited from the enhanced cascade catalytic efficiency of CoBDC-Fc/GOx, an additional immobilization of antibody further realized sensitive colorimetric immunoassay of PSA. We believe that the introduction of a protein trap can not only open an avenue for the study of enzyme immobilization but also uncover the veil of the interactions between proteins and nanomaterials.
